                    "title": "ARDS",
                    "content": "A-Atelectasis (Alveolar collapse), R-Refractory Hypoxemia, D-Decreased Lung Compliance, S-Surfactant cell damage",
                    "explanation": "Summarizes the pathophysiology of Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome."
                },
                "cheatSheet": {
                    "title": "Respiratory Failure Trends",
                    "points": [
                        "Early Failure: Tachycardia + Tachypnea + Respiratory Alkalosis",
                        "Late Failure: Bradycardia + Bradypnea + Respiratory Acidosis",
                        "Refractory Hypoxemia = Shunt (Needs PEEP, not just O2)"
                    ]
                },
                "referenceInfo": {
                    "anatomy": "Alveolar-capillary membrane: The thin barrier where gas exchange occurs. In ARDS, this becomes damaged and permeable.",
                    "physiology": "Inflammatory mediators (cytokines) damage the membrane, causing fluid to leak into alveoli (non-cardiogenic edema). Surfactant is deactivated, leading to collapse.",
                    "pharm": "Neuromuscular blockers (e.g., Rocuronium) may be needed for ventilator synchrony. Corticosteroids are controversial but sometimes used."
